      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2011 SIR. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "PURPOSE",
            "content": "To assess clinical outcome and technical feasibility of percutaneous vertebroplasty (PVP) in 34 patients with 37 osteoporotic vertebral compression fractures (OVCFs) with vertebral body collapse to less than one-third of the original height, termed very severe osteoporotic vertebral compression fractures (vsOVCFs)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "A prospective follow-up study was conducted using a 0-10 pain intensity numerical rating scale and the Short Form-36 General Health Survey (SF-36) quality-of-life questionnaire, completed before PVP and 7 days (pain only), 1 month, 3 months, and 12 months after PVP. Cement leakage was analyzed on postoperative computed tomography (CT) scanning. The presence of new fractures was assessed at 6 weeks and 52 weeks and when suspected clinically."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Decrease in average and worst back pain was 2.5 points and 3.0 points after 7 days, and 2.5 points and 2.9 points after 12 months. The physical and mental SF-36 summary scores were significantly increased. Incidence of cement leakage in vsOVCFs was 91.9%, which was substantially higher compared with non-vsOVCFs (n = 40) treated in the same patients (64.1%; odds ratio [OR] 6.4, 95% confidence interval [CI] 1.7-24.5, P = .004). Mean leakage volume per treated vertebra was more than twice as high (0.80 mL vs 0.32 mL; P < .001). Seventeen new OVCFs in 11 patients (32.4%) were identified. Only one (2.9%) minor complication occurred, confirming the feasibility of PVP in vsOVCFs."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Patients with painful vsOVCFs can be treated with, and benefit from, PVP. Although technically more demanding and with a higher procedural risk (ie, more frequent necessity of placement of a second needle, higher leakage incidence, and greater leakage volumes), PVP is technically feasible and should not be withheld from these patients."
          }
        ]
      },
